A||||8||Flash Box||Help!! Cailper Bolt Froze!!!||||5||
Z||000000||Flash Box||08-27-2000||12:26 PM||||I went to put new brake pads on and the cailper brake bolts are "froze" I tried all of my mechanic tricks to free them up and all I did was manage to strip my torx bit (I hope that I didn't screw up the bolt head!) Any suggestions on how to loosen these up. Thanks in advance for any help.<P>------------------<BR>"In Racing You Have A SPLIT SECOND To Do It And Six Days To Talk About It!"||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||08-27-2000||05:38 PM||||I almost hate to even suggest this - but since no one else has replied to your post - well, <P>First of all, to loosen the bolts, you realize they have to be turned <B>clockwise</B> as seen looking inboard from the outside of the wheel well.  Of course you knew that.  Sorry I even mentioned it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>   ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-27-2000||05:43 PM||||Which is just a Kansasites (Kansan?) way of saying, "Righty, tighty.  Lefty, loosey."  But you're on the other end of the bolt. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||08-27-2000||06:04 PM||||Zactly.  Dang, I <B>told</B> you Hoosiers was much more smarter than us Kansans (and the rest)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>And of course, we tend to use the word "clockwise" instead of "righty."  We're familiar with it because we have to reset our stupid clocks twice a year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !    ||||reg||12
Z||000004||ggcom||08-27-2000||09:01 PM||||Sorry to mention it to, but I did the same thing.. lefty loosey, righty tighty... except you have to think from the other<BR>side of that wheel hub.||||reg||11
Z||000005||deceler8||08-27-2000||10:12 PM||||You should be able to get a pair of Vice Grips on the head once you remove the rubber cap over the bolt.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mdjoneses||08-27-2000||10:16 PM||||You did try liquid wrench?  Ask at a garage they might use a propane torch on it. Mark86se2.8||||reg||2
Z||000007||fierospeeder||08-28-2000||02:30 AM||||First, take some wd40 or liquid wrench and spray the head, and remove all the dirt that is there, purpose, to get a good grip, or use something pointy to get the dirt out.<P>Get another torx bit.  Make sure that you have not stripped the head.<P>Take a breaker bar or a ratchet, and take a pipe or your jack handle and fit it in the ratchet handle, which i do.  And hold the one end from slipping while turning the pipe in the correct direction.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||08-28-2000||07:15 AM||||Stu--That's right.  We Hoosiers are definitely smarter (and more intellegent, too).  Seriously, I figure either we're smarter than most of the country in staying on the same time or else we're too stupid to figure out how to do it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
